blockspec is licensed per directory, on the same split as rowspec. This is
deliberate, not an oversight.

  SPEC.md, docs/            CC-BY-4.0
  conformance/cases/        CC0-1.0
  conformance/*.py          MIT
  reference/, tests/        Apache-2.0 OR MIT
  README.md, AGENTS.md,
  DESIGN-BRIEF.md           CC-BY-4.0
  .editorconfig,
  .gitattributes,
  .gitignore                Apache-2.0 OR MIT
  LICENSE                   CC0-1.0
  LICENSES/                 each text under its own terms, copied verbatim
  spike/*.md                CC-BY-4.0
  spike/harness/            MIT
  spike/harness/plant_cases/  CC0-1.0
  spike/harness3/           MIT
  spike/results/            CC-BY-4.0, except corpus text (below)
  spike/results/prereg3/    CC-BY-4.0, except third-party excerpts (below)

A path ending in "/" covers everything below it, subdirectories included. A
"*" matches within one directory. Where two rows cover the same path, the more
specific one applies: spike/harness/plant_cases/ is CC0-1.0 inside the MIT
spike/harness/, and spike/results/prereg3/ keeps its own row and its own
exceptions inside spike/results/.

The rows for SPEC.md, docs/, conformance/, reference/ and tests/ are decided
ahead of the files: those paths may be empty, and the split applies to whatever
lands there.

CORPUS TEXT IN spike/results/

The spike measures third-party corpora, and its results outside
spike/results/prereg3/ carry their text. In
spike/results/prereg2/export/packets/, each packet's before.md and after.md is
a whole file copied from a corpus, and its packet.json quotes from them. The
exception is the three plant packets, whose text the harness generates
(spike/harness/prereg2_plants.py). Other result files quote shorter excerpts
where they report a block, a quote or a target, merge-arm-candidates.jsonl and
cheap-arm/anchors-silent-wrongs.txt among them. The corpora and their pins are
named where each result was produced: spike/LOG.md and
spike/PRE-REGISTRATION-2.md section 6.2.

That text is not this project's work and is not offered under CC-BY-4.0. It
stays under its upstream terms, whatever they are at the pin it was taken
from. The CC-BY-4.0 row covers what this project wrote around it: the records,
figures, prompts, questions and plant texts.

THIRD-PARTY EXCERPTS IN spike/results/prereg3/

The rehearsal evidence under spike/results/prereg3/rehearsals/, the archives
included, quotes synthetic test text. That text is taken from the docstrings
of CPython 3.13 standard-library modules, as rendered by pydoc (the rehearsal
builder, rehearsals/tools/build.py, lists the modules). The pydoc output links
to docs.python.org/3.13. A few docstrings link to docs.python.org/3.11 in their
own text. These excerpts are Copyright (c) the Python Software Foundation and
contributors, and they keep their own licence, the Python Software Foundation
License Version 2 (https://docs.python.org/3.13/license.html), whose text is
in LICENSES/PSF-2.0.txt. That file is the Python 3.13 distribution's own
licence file, /usr/lib/python3.13/LICENSE.txt from Debian's
libpython3.13-stdlib 3.13.5-2+deb13u5, copied verbatim. The excerpts are not
offered under CC-BY-4.0. The builder used no other outside source. The plant
texts, prompts and harness output are this project's own.

Full texts are in LICENSES/.

WHY THE SPLIT

The point of this project is independent implementations. That makes the
reusability of the SPEC and the FIXTURES more important than the licence on any
reference code.

CommonMark embeds its conformance examples inside a CC-BY-SA spec document, so
vendoring the test cases arguably vendors share-alike prose. These fixtures are
meant to be vendored: copy them into an implementation in any language, under
any licence, without obligation.
